WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.520
What came first, illegal&nbsp;
immigration or the wall?
&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.840
The US-Mexico border is the most&nbsp;
militarized border in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.880
There is over 700 miles of fencing patrolled by&nbsp;
20,000 border patrol agents. Each year American&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:26.800
taxpayers spend over 4.8 billion dollars on Border&nbsp;
Patrol and another 8.4 billion on Immigration and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:32.000
Customs Enforcement. Is our southern border really&nbsp;
worth all of the taxes we pour into it?
&nbsp;

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:38.080
Hello, I’m Dr. Douglas Massey, I’m a Professor&nbsp;
of Sociology and Public Affairs at Princeton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.040
University and the past-president of the American&nbsp;
Academy of Political and Social Science. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.392 --> 00:00:51.040
spent my career studying unauthorized&nbsp;
immigration and the United States border.
&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:57.120
What many Americans might not realize is just&nbsp;
how new all of this border security is. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:03.040
early years of our republic there was no firm&nbsp;
border line, only a fuzzy western "frontier."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.920
As the frontier pushed westward, the border&nbsp;
between America and its neighbors continued to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.240
blurred. When the southern border was finalized&nbsp;
in the mid 1800s, it was soft and porous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.040
an invisible line on a map that ran through&nbsp;
the middle of towns and even houses
&nbsp;

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.400
In the early 1900s, labor shortages led&nbsp;
many American farmers to begin recruiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.680
fieldworkers from Mexico. The work was seasonal&nbsp;
though, with workers coming from Mexico for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.829
planting and harvest, and then returning to&nbsp;
Mexico for the off seasons. The US government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.829 --> 00:01:34.172
actually encouraged this migration through a&nbsp;
temporary labor program which helped farmers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.171 --> 00:01:36.880
recruit legal workers from Mexico.
Because they only stayed for the harvest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.160
Mexican workers were not considered immigrants and&nbsp;
weren’t included in early immigration laws that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.920
put quota limits on other nations. The US Border&nbsp;
Patrol was established in 1924, not to prevent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:53.680
the entry of Mexicans but to catch Europeans&nbsp;
trying to get around quotas at Ellis Island.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.880
During the Second World War, the U.S. government&nbsp;
once again helped farmers recruit workers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:05.280
from Mexico. The Bracero Program was created in&nbsp;
1942 and steadily expanded after the war. By the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:11.120
late 1950s, it was recruiting around 450,000&nbsp;
Mexicans per year for temporary farm labor in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:12.560
the United States; and each year another 50,000&nbsp;
or so entered as legal permanent immigrants.
&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.880
Everything changed in 1965 when&nbsp;
the Bracero program was ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:22.240
and a numerical limit on Mexican immigration was&nbsp;
imposed for the first time. Almost overnight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:26.960
Mexico went from having access to around half&nbsp;
a million legal visas, to less than 20,000.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.480
But the demand for agriculture labor didn’t&nbsp;
go away, and what was now “unauthorized” or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:38.160
“illegal” migration rose to fill the gap&nbsp;
left by the end of the Bracero Program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:46.320
Border apprehensions went from 40,000 a&nbsp;
year in 1965 to over 1.6 million in 1986.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.600
However, these were still seasonal&nbsp;
workers, and the flow was still circular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.600
with 86% of undocumented workers returning&nbsp;
home at the end of each harvest season.
&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.360
In practical terms, little had changed&nbsp;
in the Mexican-American migration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.161
system. The same workers were coming in the&nbsp;
same numbers to the same U.S. farms in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.161 --> 00:03:00.960
same states and returning to their homes in&nbsp;
Mexico at the same time each year. In symbolic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.240
terms though, much had changed, for now&nbsp;
the majority of migrants were "illegal"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:11.920
and thus by definition considered "criminals"&nbsp;
and "lawbreakers." While the demand for seasonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:16.880
agricultural work stayed the same, what the&nbsp;
government did was criminalize its workers. 
&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.680
Reacting to this perceived change,&nbsp;
the US government began pouring more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:28.080
and more money and resources into Border Patrol.&nbsp;
From 1986 to 2010, the number of uniformed Border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:35.120
Patrol officers increased 5.6 times, the&nbsp;
agency's budget grew by a factor of 17,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:40.480
and hundreds of miles of walls and fencing were&nbsp;
constructed near major ports of entry.
&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.920
None of this extra border security though did&nbsp;
anything to stop border crossings. During the 70s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.560
and 80s the odds of a migrant being apprehended&nbsp;
while crossing the border was about one in three.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:56.560
As more resources were poured into Border Patrol,&nbsp;
migrants began crossing in more remote parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.880
of the border and the odds of a migrant being&nbsp;
apprehended actually went down for a time.
&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:05.520
The diversion of border crossings into remote&nbsp;
areas lowered the odds of apprehension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:11.360
but it also increased the risk of injury&nbsp;
and death, for in addition to being less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:17.840
populated and less patrolled, these desolate&nbsp;
areas were also more dangerous. Between 1993&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.861
and 2010, the number of deaths along the border&nbsp;
skyrocketed, increasing more than five times.
&nbsp;

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:24.000
Criminalizing border crossing also made it more…&nbsp;
criminal. “Coyotes” and other smugglers moved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.520
to capitalize on the new black market and&nbsp;
border crossing became more expensive. 
&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.360
In the two decades after 1993,&nbsp;
the cost of hiring a Coyote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:39.760
increased by a factor of 4.6 in real terms.
But the biggest impact of increased border&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.720
security was that for the first time in&nbsp;
the history of Mexican-American migration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:49.840
the movement of migrants stopped being circular.&nbsp;
Finding it increasingly difficult, dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:56.160
and expensive to move back and forth for seasonal&nbsp;
work, migrants began staying in America during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:01.680
off seasons. As men stayed longer north of the&nbsp;
border, they were increasingly joined by wives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.960
and children, transitioning from seasonal&nbsp;
migrants to permanent immigrants.  
&nbsp;

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:10.800
In other words, the unintended effect of&nbsp;
restrictive immigration and border policies was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:15.760
the creation of the undocumented population as we&nbsp;
know it today. Removing the Bracero Program made a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.240
traditional, migratory economic system illegal&nbsp;
. Building walls on the southern border didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:25.920
keep people out, but rather trapped them in.
Since 2007, the number of Undocumented immigrants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:31.280
in America had actually decreased, not because&nbsp;
of the efforts of Border Patrol or ICE, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.040
rather because of an unrelated shift&nbsp;
in Mexican migration patterns.
&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.800
In the last 13 years, more people have moved from&nbsp;
the U.S. to Mexico than from Mexico to the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:46.880
This shift was started by the 2008 Recession, but&nbsp;
has been continued by a change in Mexico’s age&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:52.400
demographics. People tend to migrate when they are&nbsp;
younger, if they do not move before the age of 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:57.040
they are unlikely to do so later in life. In&nbsp;
the last 50 years, Mexico’s fertility rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:04.800
has dropped from 7.2 to 2.1 and the median age&nbsp;
of the Mexican population has gone from 16.7&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:12.000
to 29.3. Mexico has become an aging country
New undocumented immigrants in recent years have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.960
come not from Mexico, but from other countries.&nbsp;
Today, most undocumented immigrants enter the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.480
United States legally. They come with papers.&nbsp;
They enter as tourists and overstay their visas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:26.240
Border security doesn’t affect them. And yet&nbsp;
still, to this day, there are calls to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:31.600
border patrol staffing, to build more barriers,&nbsp;
to “strengthen our border defense.” Why?
&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:36.480
No matter which way you stand on the issue,&nbsp;
there are better ways to address undocumented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:47.840
immigration than a largely symbolic, totally&nbsp;
ineffective, and extremely expensive border wall.

